  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Wednesday: While the overall pattern is a ridge\nover top the Pacific Northwest. A weak shortwave will be \npulling across the region on Tuesday. Precipitable water amounts\nwill increase to 0.5-0.6 of an inch or 120-140% of normal. It \nis expected to only increase cloud amounts as precip chances \nstill remain less than 10% through Tuesday. The ridge briefly \nrebounds behind the shortwave, as a trough starts to dig along \nthe coast on Wednesday. Fog is still expected impact portions of\nthe Inland Northwest but confidence is low on where and how low\nvisibility will be. Highs will be in the upper 40s to low 50s. \nOvernight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s.\n\nThursday and Friday: The trough will push across the region \nbringing mainly Cascade precip as the rain shadow effect will \nlimit precip across the rest of the region. With snow levels \naround 4500 feet, Cascades passes are expected to get light \nsnow. Washington Pass has a 25-75 probability of 1 to 2 inches. \nStevens is less with a Trace to 1 inches. Sprinkles are possible\nacross the rest of the region. Another wave will quickly follow\non Friday with more snow for the passes. Washington has a 1-3 \ninch spread. Stevens has a Trace to one inch expected. Highs \ncontinue in the upper 40s to low 50s. Overnight lows stay in the\nupper 20s and 30s.\n\nSaturday through Monday: Long term outlook continues to show a \ndecent ridge pattern over Western Conus. Main difference is how \nthe axis sets up. It is around 50/50 split on a north-south \norientation along the coast or a southwest-northeast orientation\nfrom California to Montana. The former brings a dry, mild trend\nacross the region. The latter brings a continued shower pattern\nfor the mountain areas especially the Cascades. Highs and lows \ncontinue to trend from past days. /JDC",
